text:
Generally speaking, Spring Security interprets the declaration of a bean to mean that all filter chains should pick it up. 

Otherwise, an application uses `ServerHttpSecurity` or constructs a `WebFilterChainProxy` directly so that a given configuration applies only to that filter chain. If the firewall is set in this way, then it wouldn't be the concern of any other `WebFilterChainProxy`, like the one created [here](https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/blob/main/module/spring-boot-cloudfoundry/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/cloudfoundry/autoconfigure/actuate/endpoint/reactive/CloudFoundryReactiveActuatorAutoConfiguration.java#L182):

```java
MatcherSecurityWebFilterChain ignoredRequestFilterChain = new MatcherSecurityWebFilterChain(
		cloudFoundryRequestMatcher, Collections.singletonList(noOpFilter));
MatcherSecurityWebFilterChain allRequestsFilterChain = new MatcherSecurityWebFilterChain(
		ServerWebExchangeMatchers.anyExchange(), Collections.singletonList(existing));
return new WebFilterChainProxy(ignoredRequestFilterChain, allRequestsFilterChain);
```

So, I believe it follows that if the firewall is declared as a bean as @alfein mentioned, the intent of the application is that it be applied to all `WebFilterChainProxy` instances and can otherwise be ignored. Are there other cases you have in mind, @philwebb?

